  "page1": {
    "title": "Numbers That Tell the Middle",
    "subtitle": "Find the average, then guess how likely things are.",
    "body": "Mina has three game scores. They are 8, 9, and 10. She wants one number for the middle. That number is the <u>average</u>.\n\nTo find it, she adds the scores. 8 plus 9 plus 10 makes 27. Then she divides by the count. There are three scores, so she divides 27 by 3. The answer is 9. So the average is 9.\n\nThe average tells us the middle of the data. It is a fair way to look at many numbers at once.\n\nSome things are certain, like the sun rising. Some things are likely, and some are unlikely. Other things are impossible, like a fish that can read. Math helps us see the middle and <mark>how likely it is</mark>.",
    "illustration": "assets/illustrations/mars/2026-07/18.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "Three scores added into 27, divided by 3, landing on the average 9."
  },
